@startuml hot-spot-distribution
title US-12: H3 Selectivity - Historical index vs live candidate reduction

skinparam sequenceArrowThickness 1.5
skinparam participantBorderColor #555555
skinparam participantBackgroundColor #F8F8F8
skinparam noteBorderColor #AAAAAA
skinparam noteBackgroundColor #FFFDE7

participant "Position\nConsumer" as Consumer
database "TimescaleDB\nTime Chunk\n(observed_at partition)" as TimeChunk
database "Redis\ngeo-cell:A" as CellA
database "Redis\ngeo-cell:B" as CellB
database "Redis\ngeo-cell:C" as CellC
participant "Correlation\nWorker" as Correlation

Consumer -> Consumer : compute history_geo_cell\nand live_geo_cell
Consumer -> TimeChunk : INSERT position_history\ngeo_cell = history_geo_cell

note over TimeChunk
  All rows for the same time interval can live
  in the same TimescaleDB time chunk regardless of H3 cell.
  geo_cell is indexed and narrows queries inside the chunk.
  H3 does NOT distribute writes into separate TimescaleDB chunks.
end note

Consumer -> CellA : ZADD nearby entity memberships
Consumer -> CellB : ZADD nearby entity memberships
Consumer -> CellC : ZADD nearby entity memberships

note over CellA, CellC
  Live H3 cells are separate Redis sorted sets.
  This is where spatial bucketing reduces
  the candidate population for proximity detection.
end note

Correlation -> CellA : query current + k-ring cells\nZRANGEBYSCORE fresh members
Correlation -> CellB : query as required
Correlation -> CellC : query as required

note over Correlation
  H3 reduces candidates only.
  Exact distance decides whether a pair
  is actually within PROXIMITY_THRESHOLD_METRES.
end note

@enduml
